{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "The World of Entertainment"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Acting Jitters"}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 17}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.1"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe situations in spoken interactions according to work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Writes effective texts in English showing good organization and appropriate use of language to create the intended effect for the purpose and context.  Writes well organized texts through the correct use of formality expressions, grammar, and coherence and cohesion aspects appropriate for this level."}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn9le17ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " The Present Perfect Tense vs. The Simple Past Tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the <span class=\"strong\">Present Perfect tense</span> to talk about a past action or an action that began in the past that is connected to the present in some way. <br/> You use the <span class=\"strong\">Simple Past tense</span> to talk about many kinds of past events: short, quickly finished actions, longer situations and repeated events. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can use the <span class=\"strong\">Present Perfect tense </span> to talk about experience. When you use the <span class=\"strong\">Present Perfect tense</span>, you think about the past and present together. There is some connection between what happened in the past and now."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example: "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">I have learned English. </span> = I learned English in the past and now I can speak English.<br/> <span class=\"italic\">He has bought a painting.</span>  = He bought the painting in the past and now he has a painting."}, {"type": "text", "content": "It is common to use <span class=\"italic\">for </span> and <span class=\"italic\">since </span> when referring to time periods in the <span class=\"strong\">Present Perfect</span>:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul> <li>You use FOR to talk about how long an action has been happening (a week, 2 days, five hours, six years etc.)</li> <li>You use SINCE to talk about when the action started (2010, Monday, she was a child)</li> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"> I've tried them all. It's been a little better <span class=\"strong\">since</span> .</span> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Usually, when you use the <span class=\"strong\">Simple Past tense</span> , the time of the finished action is clear from the context or explicitly mentioned in the sentence."}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Yesterday</span> , I went to the store. <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Before</span>  they walked onto the stage and faced their audience ...</span> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "It is clear when the action occurred. In this sentence we would not use the <span class=\"strong\">Present Perfect</span>. <span class=\"italic\">(<strike>Yesterday, I have been to the store</strike>).</span> "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Have you ever felt nervous speaking in front of your class or some other group of people? "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Most actors have felt this to varying degrees before they walked onto the stage and faced their audience. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some have even asked someone backstage to give them a push when they felt more stage fright than usual. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I did that once."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've eaten many a banana before opening night! I've tried them all. It's been a little better since."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": " Making and Responding to Suggestions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": " You can use <span class=\"strong\">modals </span> or the <span class=\"strong\">zero conditional</span> to make suggestions. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Common phrases with <span class=\"strong\">modals</span> you use to make suggestions are:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">Do you think you might ...? <br/> You could ... <br/> You should ...</span> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You can also use the <span class=\"strong\">zero conditional</span> to make suggestions in a more indirect way. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">When your stomach has butterflies, a banana helps.</span> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "The structure of the <span class=\"strong\">zero conditional </span> is:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "If + <span class=\"strong\">Simple Present</span>, <span class=\"strong\">Simple Present</span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You should try imagining the crowd is naked! "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Do you think you might find a place to stretch?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "You could always chew gum to ease the tension in your jaw as well. "}, {"type": "example", "content": " When your stomach has butterflies, a banana helps."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "in public"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "in a place where you can be seen by many people "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Most people get nervous if they speak in public."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you use to talk about being nervous when speaking."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to varying degrees"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "not all at the same levels or abilities"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Most actors have felt this to varying degrees before they walked onto the stage and faced their audience."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "push"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to use force to move someone or something forward or away from you"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some have even asked someone backstage to give them a push when they felt more stage fright than usual."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "stage fright"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a nervous feeling felt by someone who is going to appear in front of an audience"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some actors felt more stage fright than usual."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to memorize"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to learn something so well that you are able to remember it perfectly"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They have to memorize lines."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "live"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "at the actual time that something (such as a performance or event) happens"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: They are also doing the show live."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to have butterflies"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a nervous feeling in your stomach"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:  When your stomach has butterflies, a banana helps."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn9le17ob09aud07"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Read the text again."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Do some research to find some other ways you can help yourself not feel nervous when speaking in front of people."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Try to come up with 4 more ways."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Then, write a dialogue between two people. One asks for advice on how to help stay calm when speaking. The other person suggests ways to help stay calm when speaking. The first person responds to the suggestions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Practice both parts."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Read your dialogue with a partner."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Change parts and read it again."}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I read the text again."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I find ways to not be nervous when speaking in front of people."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write a dialogue between two people about ways to not be nervous when speaking in front of people."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I use modal verbs to make suggestions."}, {"type": "text", "content": "I respond to suggestions."}]}], "labs": [{"exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": 